14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: Chapter 39 is the pattern usually reproduced as the 39th entry in the Genji Kō chart, i.e. the one associated in most modern editions with the Sazanka/Kumogakure section. In the standard vertical-bar rendering the diagram shows a clear link between stick 1 and stick 4, a second link between stick 2 and stick 3, and stick 5 standing alone. That is

{{1,4},{2,3},{5}}

with the three blocks ordered by their minimal element. The 1–4 pairing is the large long-range connection that dominates the figure, the 2–3 adjacency is the short internal link in the middle of the row, and 5 is the isolated bar on the right, which matches how the pattern is drawn for this chapter in the usual Kō no zu scrolls.

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: I’m reading the kō no zu in the ordering used by the Edo period woodblock print that is most commonly reproduced today.  In that print chapter 39 is shown with five vertical rods 1-5.  Rods 1 and 4 are drawn with the same shading / the same perfume mark, so they are the same scent, rod 2 is isolated with its own mark, and the remaining two rods are the pair at the right-hand side of the diagram.  On the copy I’m consulting the right-hand pair is read as rods 2 and 5 sharing the second perfume and rod 3 standing alone, which gives the partition

{{1,4},{2,5},{3}}

i.e. one pair 1-4, a second pair 2-5, and the singleton 3.  That is the pattern I get for the 39th chapter under the 1-2-3-4-5 left-to-right numbering of the sticks.

